{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-Eviction Act criminal-offense eviction grounds","requirement":"A landlord may evict a tenant convicted of, or civilly liable for, assault, threats, theft, drug activity or human trafficking on the premises, or who knowingly houses such a person; suit must be filed within two years and a three-day Notice to Quit served.","key_value":"3-day Notice to Quit; suit within 2 years","coverage_conditions":"Residential tenancies under the Anti-Eviction Act; eviction allowed only on the listed criminal, juvenile-delinquency or civil-liability grounds.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Eviction is based on the tenant's conviction, guilty plea, juvenile delinquency or civil liability for the listed offenses, or on knowingly permitting such a person to reside on the premises.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":"A tenant who allows a juvenile found delinquent for drug use or possession to reside at the property is not covered by the civil-court drug ground.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.1","quoted_span":"No eviction suit may be brought more than two years after: the juvenile was found to be delinquent; conviction of the person; or after the person’s release from incarceration, whichever is later.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Eviction from owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings","requirement":"Tenants of owner-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only by court order for listed causes such as holding over, nonpayment of rent, disorderly conduct, damage, or violation of written rules or lease terms, on a three-month or one-month notice to quit.","key_value":"3-month or 1-month notice to quit","coverage_conditions":"Rental dwellings of two or three units that the landlord occupies.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"covered","who":"owner-occupied two- or three-family dwelling","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":"Notice period depends on the tenancy: three months for at-will or year-to-year tenancies, one month for month-to-month tenancies; a three-day Notice to Quit applies to disorderly, destructive or rule- or lease-violating causes.","coverage_quotes":["Tenants of landlord-occupied two- and three-family dwellings can be removed only when a court issues an order for eviction."]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.2","quoted_span":"A one (1) month notice to quit is required for a month-to-month tenancy and other types of tenancies are entitled to a one (1) month notice to quit.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Rooming and boarding house eviction requirements","requirement":"Owners of rooming and boarding houses must follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, and must give the County Welfare Board at least three working days' notice before starting an eviction action.","key_value":"3 working days' notice to the County Welfare Board","coverage_conditions":"Buildings with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms, unless they meet an exception in the Rooming and Boarding House Act.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","text":"building with at least two living units occupied by unrelated persons without private kitchens and bathrooms"}],"per_tenancy":"The three-working-day notice to the County Welfare Board applies before the owner starts an eviction action for any resident.","coverage_quotes":["Any building having at least two (2) living units occupied by persons unrelated to each other without private kitchens and bathro oms is a rooming or boarding house if it does not meet one (1) of the exceptions in the Rooming and Boarding House Act"]},"exemptions":"Hotels with more than 85 percent temporary occupancy by people with homes elsewhere, school and college dormitories, buildings housing only college students and certain residences for the disabled.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3","quoted_span":"The Regulations Governing Rooming and Boarding Houses, which are enforced by the Bureau of Rooming and Boarding House Standards of the Department of Community Affairs, require owners of rooming and boarding houses to follow the good causes and notice requirements of the Eviction Law when evicting residents, except if otherwise ordered by the Bureau (N.J.A.C. 5:27-3.3(c)).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Penalties for eviction law violations","requirement":"A landlord who took possession for personal occupancy must occupy the unit at least six months, and must not return a unit evicted for demolition or permanent retirement from residential use to residential use for five years; violations make the landlord liable to the former tenant.","key_value":"3x damages plus attorney fees and costs; civil penalty up to $10,000","coverage_conditions":"Tenants who vacate after a notice of the landlord's personal occupancy or of permanent removal of the building from residential use.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies where the tenant vacated after a notice that the landlord would personally occupy the unit, or that the building would be permanently boarded up, demolished or retired from residential use.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":"Three times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs; for the demolition or retirement ground, also a civil penalty up to $10,000 for each violation and no planned real estate development registration for five years.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.6","quoted_span":"If instead the landlord permits personal occupancy of the unit by another tenant or registration of conversion of the property to a condominium or cooperative, the landlord is liable to the former tenant for three (3) times the damages suffered plus attorney fees and costs","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Anti-reprisal protections for tenants","requirement":"A landlord may not evict, substantially alter a lease or refuse to renew a lease in reprisal because a tenant enforced lease or legal rights, made a good-faith complaint to a government authority, joined a tenant organization or refused unlawful lease changes.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"All rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"owner","role":"exempt","who":"owner-occupied two- or three-family dwelling","unit_limit":3}],"per_tenancy":"Applies when the tenant exercises the listed civil rights, including enforcing lease or State or local law rights, making a good-faith complaint to a governmental authority after notifying the landlord, being an organizer or member of a lawful tenant organization, or refusing lease changes made in retaliation.","coverage_quotes":["The law against reprisal applies to all rental properties used for dwelling purposes, including mobile homes, except owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings."]},"exemptions":"Owner-occupied two- or three-family dwellings.","penalty":"The tenant may sue the landlord for damages in a civil action.","effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10","quoted_span":"A landlord cannot take reprisal action against a tenant by eviction, substantial alteration of a lease or its terms, or refusal to renew a lease when a tenant exercises certain civil rights (N.J.S.A. 2A:42-10.10).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act","requirement":"Eligible senior, disabled and qualifying disabled-veteran tenants in buildings being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership may be protected from eviction for 40 years, with an income limit of three times the county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater.","key_value":"40 years' protection; income not more than 3x county average per-person income or $50,000, whichever is greater","coverage_conditions":"Tenants at least 62 before the conversion recording, permanently disabled, or honorably discharged veterans disabled 60% or more from service, who lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording date.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Tenant must meet the age, disability or veteran criteria and have lived in the building at least one year before the conversion recording date; protection runs 40 years if family income is within the limit.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.22 et seq.","quoted_span":"Tenants may be protected from eviction for 40 years if their family income is not more than three (3) times the average per person income in their county or $50,000.00, whichever  is greater","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.8,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Tenant Protection Act of 1992 (Hudson County conversion protections)","requirement":"Extends protected-tenancy protections from eviction to qualified tenants in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible under the Senior Citizens and Disabled Protected Tenancy Act, in qualified counties.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Buildings converted or being converted to condominium, cooperative or fee simple ownership in a qualified county; currently only Hudson County.","applicability":{"conditions":[{"type":"other","text":"building converted or being converted in a qualified county (currently only Hudson County)"}],"per_tenancy":"Tenant qualifies if not eligible for protected tenancy as a senior citizen or disabled person under the 1981 Act.","coverage_quotes":["The Tenant Protection Law of 1992 amendment extends protections to qualified tenants in qualified counties in buildings converted or being converted who were not eligible for Protected Tenancy as either Senior Citizens or Disabled Persons"]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:18-61.40 et seq.","quoted_span":"At the present time, the only qualified county is Hudson County (N.J.A.C. 5:24-3.2(b)).","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.75,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"just_cause_eviction","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Foreclosure eviction protections and notices","requirement":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction; before evicting a tenant of foreclosed property for the buyer's personal occupancy the landlord must give a 90-day notice to quit, and a person acquiring the property must notify tenants within 10 business days after the sale.","key_value":"90-day notice to quit; 10-business-day notice after sale","coverage_conditions":"Residential rental units in foreclosed properties, including month-to-month tenancies and two- and three-family owner-occupied units.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies when a foreclosed property is bought by a buyer who wants to personally occupy it as a primary residence, and when a person acquires a foreclosed property with residential rental units.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":"None stated; month-to-month tenants and two- and three-family owner-occupied units are not exempt from the 90-day notice.","penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":"N.J.S.A. 2A:50-69 et seq.","quoted_span":"Foreclosure alone is not grounds for eviction in New Jersey. In Chase Manhattan Bank v. Josephson, 135 N.J. 209 (1994) the court held that when a lender or other buyer takes possession of the property, the residential tenant comes with the property.","interaction":"Federal law does not preempt any State or local law that provides longer time periods or other additional protections for tenants in foreclosure proceedings.","relations":[],"confidence":0.7,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
{"packet_id":"D067-04","doc_id":"D067","jurisdiction":"NJ","category":"rent_increase_limits","lifecycle":"enacted","title":"Harassment by excessive or unreasonable rent increase","requirement":"A landlord's harassment of a tenant includes a rent increase above any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or, where the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","key_value":null,"coverage_conditions":"Rental dwellings in New Jersey; for rent-controlled properties the increase is measured against the local rent control or rent leveling ordinance.","applicability":{"conditions":[],"per_tenancy":"Applies to a rent increase charged to a tenant that exceeds a rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or that is unreasonable or unconscionable where no rent control applies.","coverage_quotes":[]},"exemptions":null,"penalty":null,"effective_date":null,"valid_through":null,"citation":null,"quoted_span":"An increase in rent in excess of any rent control or rent leveling ordinance, or if the property is not subject to rent control, an unreasonable or unconscionable rent increase.","interaction":null,"relations":[],"confidence":0.5,"conflict_flag":false,"conflict_note":null}
